&quot;Based on the analysis of the project files, I now have a complete understanding of the project structure and how it works.\n\n### WebGL Visualization Project Analysis\n\nThis is a simple WebGL project that demonstrates 3D graphics rendering in a web browser. The project shows a 3D visualization with mouse rotation controls (trackball rotation).\n\n## Project Structure\n\n- **index.html** - The main HTML file that contains the canvas element and loads all JavaScript files\n- **main.js** - The core application logic with initialization, drawing, and model creation\n- **shader.gpu** - Contains vertex and fragment shader code for WebGL rendering\n- **Utils/** - Directory with utility files:\n  - **trackball-rotator.js** - Handles mouse interaction for rotating the 3D scene\n  - **m4.js** - Matrix mathematics utility for 3D transformations\n\n## How the Project Works\n\nThe project creates a simple 3D visualization (a cylinder-like surface) that can be rotated using the mouse. The main components work together as follows:\n\n1. The **index.html** file loads all scripts and defines a canvas with ID \&quot;webglcanvas\&quot;\n2. The **main.js** file:\n   - Initializes WebGL context\n   - Sets up shaders\n   - Creates 3D model data\n   - Handles the rendering loop\n3. The **shader.gpu** file contains GLSL code for vertex and fragment shaders\n4. The utility files provide support for 3D transformations and user interaction\n\n## How to Run the Project\n\nSince this is a WebGL project, you need a web browser to run it. Here\u0027s how:\n\n1. **Using a Local Web Server (Recommended):**\n   - Start a local web server in the project directory\n   - Open a browser and navigate to the server address (typically http://localhost:8000)\n\n2. **Using File Protocol (Simple but Less Secure):**\n   - Open the index.html file directly in a modern web browser that supports WebGL\n\n### Setting Up a Local Web Server\n\nThere are several options for setting up a local web server:\n\n**Option 1: Using Python (if you have Python installed)**\n\n```bash\n# For Python 3:\npython -m http.server\n\n# For Python 2:\npython -m SimpleHTTPServer\n```\n\n**Option 2: Using Node.js and http-server (if you have Node.js installed)**\n\n```bash\n# Install http-server globally (one-time setup)\nnpm install -g http-server\n\n# Start server\nhttp-server\n```\n\n**Option 3: Using Visual Studio Code Live Server extension**\n- Install the \&quot;Live Server\&quot; extension in VS Code\n- Right-click on index.html and select \&quot;Open with Live Server\&quot;\n\n## Features\n\n- 3D visualization of a simple geometric shape\n- Interactive \&quot;trackball\&quot; rotation using mouse (click and drag)\n- Works on touchscreens as well (drag with finger)\n\n## Requirements\n\n- A modern web browser with WebGL support (Chrome, Firefox, Edge, Safari)\n- No other dependencies or installations needed\n\nThe project is a straightforward WebGL demonstration that serves as a foundation for more complex 3D visualizations. According to the README, it\u0027s part of a VGGI credit module, with more information available at vggi-kpi.blogspot.com.&quot;}]}]},&quot;e20da62c-6771-40a4-a8ca-95654123aca1&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;e20da62c-6771-40a4-a8ca-95654123aca1&quot;,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;WebGL Wireframe Rendering of the Cornucopia Surface (Variant)&quot;,&quot;timestamp&quot;:1766669181335,&quot;messages&quot;:[{&quot;role&quot;:&quot;user&quot;,&quot;content&quot;:[{&quot;type&quot;:&quot;text&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;Complete task here \nusing webgl and js\n\nRequirements\nBasing on the skeleton project add a new js script file containing Model object.\nModel object has to draw the surface wireframe as two sets of vertices: a set of U polylines and a set of V polylines.\nFigure out your order number in a list of the group you study at in campus system, that is your variant\nRender wire-frame model representing analytical surface using WebGL as per individual variant\nCreate 