ornucopia3.js-335-\nD:\\PROJECT_ME\\project_SHAME\\INSTITUTKA\\Visualisation\\WebGL2\\cornucopia3.js-336-/**\n[Showing results with pagination \u003d limit: 100, offset: 0]&quot;},{&quot;role&quot;:&quot;assistant&quot;,&quot;content&quot;:[{&quot;type&quot;:&quot;text&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;Let me check the values for the lightPosition and lightRadius variables:&quot;}],&quot;tool_calls&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;toolu_01Q2nWdmKxJSTexU95KNvsk2&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;function&quot;,&quot;function&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;grep&quot;,&quot;arguments&quot;:&quot;{\&quot;pattern\&quot;: \&quot;let.*lightRadius\&quot;, \&quot;path\&quot;: \&quot;D:/PROJECT_ME/project_SHAME/INSTITUTKA/Visualisation/WebGL2/cornucopia3.js\&quot;, \&quot;output_mode\&quot;: \&quot;content\&quot;}&quot;}}]},{&quot;role&quot;:&quot;tool&quot;,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;grep&quot;,&quot;tool_call_id&quot;:&quot;toolu_01Q2nWdmKxJSTexU95KNvsk2&quot;,&quot;content&quot;:&quot;D:\\PROJECT_ME\\project_SHAME\\INSTITUTKA\\Visualisation\\WebGL2\\cornucopia3.js:22:let lightRadius \u003d 60;   // Distance of light from origin\n[Showing results with pagination \u003d limit: 100, offset: 0]&quot;},{&quot;role&quot;:&quot;assistant&quot;,&quot;content&quot;:[],&quot;tool_calls&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;toolu_019UxiPLqQEr2AYVDbaNd2Hg&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;function&quot;,&quot;function&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;grep&quot;,&quot;arguments&quot;:&quot;{\&quot;pattern\&quot;: \&quot;lightZ\&quot;, \&quot;path\&quot;: \&quot;D:/PROJECT_ME/project_SHAME/INSTITUTKA/Visualisation/WebGL2/cornucopia3.js\&quot;, \&quot;output_mode\&quot;: \&quot;content\&quot;}&quot;}}]},{&quot;role&quot;:&quot;tool&quot;,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;grep&quot;,&quot;tool_call_id&quot;:&quot;toolu_019UxiPLqQEr2AYVDbaNd2Hg&quot;,&quot;content&quot;:&quot;D:\\PROJECT_ME\\project_SHAME\\INSTITUTKA\\Visualisation\\WebGL2\\cornucopia3.js:410:    const lightZ \u003d 50; // Height of the light\n--\nD:\\PROJECT_ME\\project_SHAME\\INSTITUTKA\\Visualisation\\WebGL2\\cornucopia3.js:412:    return [lightX, lightY, lightZ];\n[Showing results with pagination \u003d limit: 100, offset: 0]&quot;},{&quot;role&quot;:&quot;assistant&quot;,&quot;content&quot;:[{&quot;type&quot;:&quot;text&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;Now I understand what\u0027s happening. Let me explain why you can see the dot of light (light source) in your scene.\n\n### Why You Can See the Light Source\n\nYou\u0027re not actually seeing the light source itself as a rendered object. What you\u0027re seeing is a **very intense specular highlight** that gives the appearance of a visible light source. Here\u0027s why it happens:\n\n1. **High Specular Value**: In your code, the specular component is set quite high:\n   ```javascript\n   gl.uniform3fv(shaderProgram.iMaterialSpecular, [0.8, 0.8, 0.8]);\n   gl.uniform1f(shaderProgram.iShininess, 30.0);\n   ```\n   This creates a strong specular reflection (shiny spot) on your surface.\n\n2. **Direct Reflection Angle**: When the camera angle and the surface orientation align perfectly with the light source position, you get a very bright specular highlight that looks like a dot or point of light.\n\n3. **Light Position**: Your light source is positioned relatively far from the model:\n   ```javascript\n   let lightRadius \u003d 60;   // Distance of light from origin\n   const lightZ \u003d 50;     // Height of the light\n   ```\n   This distance creates a more concentrated specular highlight that appears as a distinct dot.\n\n4. **Rotating Light**: Since your light is constantly rotating around the model (via the `updateLightPosition()` function), this bright specular highlight moves across the surface, creating the illusion of seeing the light source itself moving.\n\n### Technical Explanation\n\nIn Phong shading, the specular component is calculated as:\n```\nspecular \u003d materialSpecular * pow(max(dot(viewDir, reflectDir), 0.0), shininess)\n```\n\nWhen the reflection direction (`reflectDir`) perfectly aligns with the view direction (`viewDir`), their dot product becomes 1.0, which, raised to any power (your `shininess` is 30.0), remains 1.0. This creates the maximum possible specular reflection at that point, resulting in a bright spot that looks like the light source itself.\n\n### To Make the Light Source Less Visible\n\nIf you want to reduce this effect, you can:\n\n1. Reduce the specular component values:\n   ```javascript\n   gl.uniform3fv(shaderProgram.iMaterialSpecular, [0.3, 0.3, 0.3]); // Lower values\n   ```\n\n2. Increase the shininess for a smaller, more concentrated highlight:\n   ```javascript\n   gl.uniform1f(shaderProgram.iShininess, 100.0); // Higher value \u003d smaller highlight\n   ```\n\nWould you like me to implement these changes to make the light source less visible?&quot;}]},{&quot;role&quot;:&quot;user&quot;,&quot;content&quot;:[{&quot;type&quot;:&quot;text&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;generate the dot\noutside of the surface\nand cast light from it&quot;}],&quot;context&quot;:[{&quot;file_path&quot;:&quot;index.html&quot;,&quot;code_text&quot;:&quot;\u003c!DOCTYPE html\u003e\r\n\u003chtml\u003e\r\n\u003chead\u003e\r\n    \u003cmeta http-equiv\u003d\&quot;Content-Type\&quot; content\u003d\&quot;text/html; charset\u003dUTF-8\&quot;\u003e\r\n    \u003ctitle\u003eCornucopia Surface - WebGL Visualization with Phong Shading\u003c/title\u003e\r\n    \u003cstyle\u003e\r\n        body {\r\n            background-color: #111;\r\n            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\r\n            color: #fff;\r\n            text-align: center;\r\n            margin: 0;\r\n            padding: 20px;\r\n        }\r\n        h1, h2 {\r\n            color: #0f0;\r\n        }\r\n        h1 {\r\n            margin-top: 10px;\r\n            font-size: 28px;\r\n        }\r\n        h2 {\r\n            font-size: 22px;\r\n            margin-top: 25px;\r\n        }\r\n        .container {\r\n            max-width: 1000px;\r\n            mar