ia surface using WebGL.\\n\\n## Assignment Requirements\\n- **Variant 23**: Prioritize tangent in Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization\\n- **Three Textures**:\\n  1. **Diffuse Texture**: Modulates the diffuse lighting component\\n  2. **Specular Texture**: Modulates the specular lighting component  \\n  3. **Normal Texture**: Contains per-pixel normal data in tangent space\\n\\n## Implementation Details\\n\\n### 1. Tangent Space Calculation\\nThe implementation calculates tangent and bitangent vectors for each vertex:\\n- Tangents are computed from UV texture coordinates and vertex positions\\n- Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization is applied with **tangent priority** (Variant 23)\\n- The TBN (Tangent, Bitangent, Normal) matrix transforms normals from tangent space to view space\\n\\n### 2. Gram-Schmidt Orthogonalization (Prioritizing Tangent)\\nFor Variant 23, we:\\n1. **Normalize the tangent** vector first\\n2. **Orthogonalize the normal** with respect to the tangent: `n\u0027 \u003d n - (n · t) * t`\\n3. **Calculate bitangent** as the cross product: `b \u003d n\u0027 × t`\\n\\nThis ensures the tangent vector remains unchanged while the normal and bitangent are adjusted to maintain orthogonality.\\n\\n### 3. Texture Mapping\\n- **Diffuse texture**: Applied to base color and modulates diffuse lighting\\n- **Specular texture**: Modulates specular highlights\\n- **Normal texture**: Provides per-pixel normal variations in tangent space\\n  - Normal map values are in [0, 1] range (RGB)\\n  - Converted to [-1, 1] range: `normal \u003d normalMap * 2.0 - 1.0`\\n  - Transformed from tangent space to view space using TBN matrix\\n\\n### 4. Shader Implementation\\n**Vertex Shader**: \\n- Passes position, normal, tangent, bitangent, and texture coordinates to fragment shader\\n- Transforms all vectors to view space\\n\\n**Fragment Shader**:\\n- Constructs TBN matrix from interpolated tangent, bitangent, and normal\\n- Samples normal from normal map texture\\n- Transforms normal to view space\\n- Applies Phong lighting with texture modulation\\n\\n## Creating Normal Maps\\n\\n### Windows (Photoshop)\\n1. Open your texture image in Photoshop\\n2. Go to **Filter → 3D → Generate Normal Map**\\n3. Adjust the settings (strength, detail)\\n4. Save as PNG or JPG\\n\\n### Linux (GIMP)\\n1. Install the normal map plugin: `sudo apt-get install gimp-normalmap`\\n2. Open your texture in GIMP\\n3. Go to **Filters → Generic → Normal Map**\\n4. Adjust settings and apply\\n5. Export as PNG\\n\\n### Online Tools\\n- **NormalMap Online**: https://cpetry.github.io/NormalMap-Online/\\n- **Normal Map Generator**: Upload a height/texture map and generate normals\\n\\n## Using Custom Textures\\n\\nTo use your own textures, modify `cornucopia3.js` in the `initGL()` function:\\n\\n```javascript\\nsurface.textureDiffuse \u003d LoadTexture(\\\&quot;path/to/your/diffuse.png\\\&quot;);\\nsurface.textureSpecular \u003d LoadTexture(\\\&quot;path/to/your/specular.png\\\&quot;);\\nsurface.textureNormal \u003d LoadTexture(\\\&quot;path/to/your/normal.png\\\&quot;);\\n```\\n\\nOr create a procedural normal map:\\n```javascript\\nsurface.textureNormal \u003d CreateFlatNormalMap(256); // Flat normal map (no bumps)\\n```\\n\\n## Testing the Implementation\\n\\n1. **Install Live Server** extension in VS Code\\n2. **Right-click** on `index.html` and select \\\&quot;Open with Live Server\\\&quot;\\n3. The browser will open showing the Cornucopia surface with normal mapping\\n4. **Drag** the mouse to rotate the view\\n5. **Adjust** U and V segment sliders to change mesh density\\n6. **Modify** surface parameters and click \\\&quot;Apply Parameters\\\&quot;\\n\\n## Key Features\\n\\n✓ Normal mapping with per-pixel normal detail  \\n✓ Three texture support (diffuse, specular, normal)  \\n✓ Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization prioritizing tangent  \\n✓ TBN matrix for tangent-space to view-space transformation  \\n✓ Phong lighting model with texture modulation  \\n✓ Interactive camera controls  \\n✓ Animated rotating light source  \\n✓ Adjustable mesh granularity  \\n✓ Dynamic parameter updates  \\n\\n## Technical Notes\\n\\n- **Coordinate System**: Right-handed coordinate system\\n- **Winding Order**: Counter-clockwise (CCW) for front faces\\n- **Normal Map Format**: RGB where (0.5, 0.5, 1.0) \u003d (0, 0, 1) in tangent space\\n- **Texture Wrapping**: CLAMP_TO_EDGE for non-power-of-2 textures\\n- **WebGL Version**: WebGL 1.0\\n\\n## Troubleshooting\\n\\n**Problem**: Normals appear inverted  \\n**Solution**: Check the normal inversion in vertex shader (currently inverted for correct shadow orientation)\\n\\n**Problem**: Textures don\u0027t load  \\n**Solution**: Ensure CORS is enabled if loading from external URLs; use local files or Live Server\\n\\n**Problem**: Performance issues  \\n**Solution**: Reduce U/V segments using the granularity sliders\\n\\n**Problem**: Normal map looks flat  \\n**Solution**: Use an actual normal map with height variations instead of the default flat map\\n\\n## Author\\nPashchenko Mykola  \\nGroup: TR-51mp  \\nVariant: 23 (Prioritize Tangent)\\n\&quot;}&quot;}}]},{&quot;role&quot;:&quot;tool&quot;,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;write&quot;,&quot;tool_call_id&quot;:&quot;toolu_01XB9bcLQVeMfUZESj8j77fc&quot;,&quot;content&quot;:&quot;Wrote contents to D:\\PROJECT_ME\\project_SHAME\\INSTITUTKA\\Visualisation\\WebGL2\\NORMAL_MAPPING_GUIDE.md.\n\nWhen you\u0027re done with your current set of changes to this file, you should call the read_lints tool with the specific file path and fix any newly introduced errors.&quot;},{&quot;role&quot;:&quot;assistant&quot;,&quot;content&quot;:[{&quot;type&quot;:&quot;thinking&quot;,&quot;thinking&quot;:&quot;Now let me run a quick test to make sure the application will work. Let me check if there are any syntax errors or issues by running the linter on all modified files.&quot;,&quot;signature&quot;:&quot;\u003d\u003d&quot;},{&quot;type&quot;:&quot;text&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;Let me verify all the mo