&quot;ErcBCkgICxgCIkCnwJqC9Uup+eZ+RUX9uWFos9OEdER9nGV9ilobqnh+ZoUuLQUPKM9Hdkp9zgubqFuY2fljPIr0w5AFYDv0OSRtKAESDC39S61of6ZVRalBVxoM6HxneDUjKL2zR493IjBSZtXVexhksccrGJX4rm8luLw4DofMlD/XEfVzjaYZ5Jt84DSJwccCmPWRLKXeoBUqHWz02r4QtclovgZAaqKjGxLQxbd6CM3SS8ne+PoYGAI\u003d&quot;},{&quot;type&quot;:&quot;text&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;I\u0027ll add the title page in Ukrainian at the beginning of the README.md file.&quot;}],&quot;tool_calls&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;toolu_01CNgkiD8xEqdAqm1wBf8JY7&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;function&quot;,&quot;function&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;read_file&quot;,&quot;arguments&quot;:&quot;{\&quot;target_file\&quot;: \&quot;D:/PROJECT_ME/project_SHAME/INSTITUTKA/Visualisation/WebGL2/README.md\&quot;}&quot;}}]},{&quot;role&quot;:&quot;tool&quot;,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;read&quot;,&quot;tool_call_id&quot;:&quot;toolu_01CNgkiD8xEqdAqm1wBf8JY7&quot;,&quot;content&quot;:&quot;  1|# WebGL Texture Transformation Project\n  2|\n  3|## Cornucopia Surface with Dynamic Texture Mapping\n  4|\n  5|![Before Transformation](screenShot/screenbefore.png)\n  6|\n  7|---\n  8|\n  9|# 1. Task Description\n 10|\n 11|## Operations on Texture Coordinates\n 12|\n 13|The task focuses on implementing advanced texture coordinate transformations in WebGL, specifically enabling dynamic manipulation of textures on a parametric surface. The primary objective is to scale or rotate a texture around a user-specified point within the UV space of the texture.\n 14|\n 15|### Requirements\n 16|\n 17|1. **Reuse existing functionality**: The implementation must reuse the texture mapping from the previous Control task as a foundation.\n 18|\n 19|2. **Transformation implementation**: \n 20|   - For odd-numbered variants: Implement texture scaling around a user-specified point\n 21|   - For even-numbered variants: Implement texture rotation around a user-specified point\n 22|\n 23|3. **Interactive control**: Enable movement of the reference point within the texture\u0027s (u,v) space using keyboard controls:\n 24|   - **A and D keys**: Move the reference point along the u parameter (horizontal)\n 25|   - **W and S keys**: Move the reference point along the v parameter (vertical)\n 26|\n 27|### Technical Focus\n 28|\n 29|This task combines several important concepts in computer graphics:\n 30|- Texture coordinate manipulation\n 31|- Real-time user interaction\n 32|- Parametric surface rendering\n 33|- Shader-based transformations\n 34|\n 35|The implementation should provide a seamless user experience that allows for dynamic visualization of how texture transformations affect the appearance of the 3D parametric surface.\n 36|\n 37|---\n 38|\n 39|# 2. Theoretical Background\n 40|\n 41|## 2.1 Texture Mapping Fundamentals\n 42|\n 43|Texture mapping is a technique in computer graphics that applies a 2D image (texture) to the surface of a 3D object. The process requires establishing a correspondence between points on the 3D model and locations within the 2D texture space.\n 44|\n 45|### Texture Coordinates\n 46|\n 47|A key concept in texture mapping is the texture coordinate system, typically denoted as (u,v) coordinates:\n 48|- The u-coordinate represents the horizontal position within the texture (0 at left, 1 at right)\n 49|- The v-coordinate represents the vertical position within the texture (0 at top, 1 at bottom)\n 50|\n 51|For parametric surfaces like the Cornucopia model used in this project, there\u0027s a natural mapping between the surface parameters and the texture coordinates, allowing for precise control of texture placement.\n 52|\n 53|### Texture Sampling\n 54|\n 55|When rendering, the graphics pipeline performs texture sampling—the process of retrieving color values from a texture based on computed texture coordinates. The quality of this sampling is influenced by:\n 56|\n 57|- **Filtering modes**: Techniques for interpolating between texture pixels\n 58|- **Mipmap levels**: Pre-computed downscaled versions of the texture for different viewing distances\n 59|- **Wrapping modes**: How textures behave outside the [0,1] coordinate range\n 60|\n 61|## 2.2 Texture Transformations in UV Space\n 62|\n 63|Transforming textures in UV space involves modifying the texture coordinates before they are used to sample the texture. Unlike transforming the 3D object itself, this approach changes only how the texture appears on the surface.\n 64|\n 65|### Basic Transformations\n 66|\n 67|The fundamental texture transformations include:\n 68|- **Translation**: Shifting the texture along the u or v axis\n 69|- **Scaling**: Expanding or contracting the texture\n 70|- **Rotation**: Rotating the texture around a specified point\n 71|\n 72|### Transformation Mathematics\n 73|\n 74|For operations like scaling and rotation around a specific point, we follow a three-step process:\n 75|\n 76|1. **Translate to origin**: Move the reference point to the origin\n 77|   ```\n 78|   uv\u0027 \u003d uv - reference_point\n 79|   ```\n 80|\n 81|2. **Apply transformation**: Perform the scaling or rotation\n 82|   - For scaling:\n 83|   ```\n 84|   uv\u0027\u0027 \u003d uv\u0027 * scale_factor\n 85|   ```\n 86|   - For rotation by angle θ:\n 87|   ```\n 88|   uv\u0027\u0027 \u003d [cos(θ) -sin(θ); sin(θ) cos(θ)] * uv\u0027\n 89|   ```\n 90|\n 91|3. **Translate back**: Return the reference point to its original position\n 92|   ```\n 93|   uv\u0027\u0027\u0027 \u003d uv\u0027\u0027 + reference_point\n 94|   ```\n 95|\n 96|### GLSL Implementation\n 97|\n 98|In WebGL, these transformations are most efficiently implemented within the vertex shader. The transformed texture coordinates are then interpolated across the triangle and used in the fragment shader for texture sampling.\n 99|\n100|## 2.3 Parametric Surface Texturing\n101|\n102|The Cornucopia surface used in this project is defined by parametric equations, which creates a natural parameterization for texture mapping:\n103|\n104|```\n105|x(u,v) \u003d [e^(mu) + e^(pu)cos(v)]cos(u)\n106|y(u,v) \u003d [e^(mu) + e^(pu)cos(v)]sin(u)\n107|z(u,v) \u003d e^(pu)sin(v)\n108|```\n109|\n110|This parametric nature allows us to directly map the surface parameters (u,v) to texture coordinates, creating a coherent texture mapping across the entire surface. The challenge lies in implementing transformations that respect this parameterization while providing intuitive controls for the user.\n111|\n112|---\n113|\n114|# 3. Implementation Details\n115|\n116|## 3.1 Architecture Overview\n117|\n118|The implementation follows a modular architecture with clear separation of concerns:\n119|\n120|![After Transformation](screenShot/screenAfter.png)\n121|\n122|- **HTML/CSS**: Provides the user interface and canvas container\n123|- **JavaScript**: Handles initialization, event handling, and scene management\n124|- **WebGL**: Renders the 3D scene with shaders\n125|- **GLSL Shaders**: Implements the core texture transformation logic\n126|\n127|## 3.2 Shader Implementation\n128|\n129|The texture transformation is implemented primarily in the vertex shader, where texture coordinates are modified before being passed to the fragment shader.\n130|\n131|### Vertex Shader Transformation Function\n132|\n133|```glsl\n134|// Function to transform texture coordinates based on scaling/rotation\n135|vec2 transformTexCoords(vec2 texCoords, vec2 refPoint, float scale, float angle) {\n136|    // Translate to reference point origin\n137|    vec2 centered \u003d texCoords - refPoint;\n138|    \n139|    // Apply scale and rotation\n140|    float s \u003d sin(angle);\n141|    float c \u003d cos(angle);\n142|    \n143|    // Create rotation matrix\n144|    mat2 rotation \u003d mat2(\n145|        c, -s,\n146|        s, c\n147|    );\n148|    \n149|    // Apply scale or rotation based on variant\n150|    vec2 transformed;\n151|    \n152|    #ifdef ENABLE_ROTATION\n153|        // Apply rotation around reference point\n154|        transformed \u003d rotation * centered;\n155|    #else\n156|        // Apply scaling around reference point\n157|        transformed \u003d centered * scale;\n158|    #endif\n159|    \n160|    // Translate back from reference point\n161|    return transformed + refPoint;\n162|}\n163|```\n164|\n165|This function encapsulates the three-step transformation process described in the theory section:\n166|1. Translate to origin by subtracting the reference point\n167|2. Apply the appropriate transformation (scaling or rotation)\n168|3. Translate back by adding the reference point\n169|\n170|The preprocessing directive `#ifdef ENABLE_ROTATION` allows for conditional compilation based on the variant number, ensuring that odd variants implement scaling while even variants implement rotation.\n171|\n172|## 3.3 Reference Point Movement\n173|\n174|The reference point movement is managed through keyboard event listeners that update the global texture transformation variables:\n175|\n176|```javascript\n177|function setupKeyboardControls() {\n178|    document.addEventListener(\u0027keydown\u0027, function(event) {\n179|        let needRedraw \u003d true;\n180|        \n181|        switch(event.key) {\n182|            case \u0027a\u0027: case \u0027A\u0027: // Move reference point left (u-)\n183|                texReferencePoint[0] -\u003d texMoveSpeed;\n184|                break;\n185|                \n186|            case \u0027d\u0027: case \u0027D\u0027: // Move reference point right (u+)\n187|                texReferencePoint[0] +\u003d texMoveSpeed;\n188|                break;\n189|                \n190|            case \u0027w\u0027: case \u0027W\u0027: // Move reference point up (v-)\n191|                texReferencePoint[1] -\u003d texMoveSpeed;\n192|                break;\n193|                \n194|            case \u0027s\u0027: case \u0027S\u0027: // Move reference point down (v+)\n195|                texReferencePoint[1] +\u003d texMoveSpeed;\n196|                break;\n197|            \n198|            // Additional controls for scaling and rotation\n199|            // ...\n200|            \n201|            default:\n202|                needRedraw \u003d false;\n203|                break;\n204|        }\n205|        \n206|        // Ensure reference point stays in valid range\n207|        texReferencePoint[0] \u003d Math.max(0.0, Math.min(1.0, texReferencePoint[0]));\n208|        texReferencePoint[1] \u003d Math.max(0.0, Math.min(1.0, texReferencePoint[1]));\n209|        \n210|        // Update UI display\n211|        updateTextureControlsDisplay();\n212|        \n213|        if (needRedraw) {\n214|            // Request a new frame to see the changes\n215|            requestAnimationFrame(draw);\n216|        }\n217|    });\n218|}\n219|```\n220|\n221|This implementation ensures that the reference point remains within the valid [0,1] texture coordinate range, and updates the UI to reflect the current values.\n222|\n223|## 3.4 Variant Detection\n224|\n225|To implement the variant-specific behavior (scaling for odd variants, rotation for even variants), the shader compilation process includes variant detection:\n226|\n227|```javascript\n228|function createProgram(gl, vertexShaderSource, fragmentShaderSource) {\n229|    // Determine the variant number from the DOM\n230|    let variantNumber \u003d 23; // Default\n231|    \n232|    try {\n233|        // Find variant number in student info section\n234|        const studentInfo \u003d document.querySelector(\&quot;.student-info\&quot;);\n235|        if (studentInfo) {\n236|            const strongElements \u003d studentInfo.querySelectorAll(\&quot;strong\&quot;);\n237|            \n238|            for (let i \u003d 0; i \u003c strongElements.length; i++) {\n239|                if (strongElements[i].textContent.includes(\&quot;Variant\&quot;)) {\n240|                    const variantText \u003d strongElements[i].parentElement.textContent;\n241|                    const match \u003d variantText.match(/Variant.*?(\\d+)/);\n242|                    if (match \u0026\u0026 match[1]) {\n243|                        variantNumber \u003d parseInt(match[1]);\n244|                        console.log(\&quot;Detected variant number:\&quot;, variantNumber);\n245|                        break;\n246|                    }\n247|                }\n248|            }\n249|        }\n250|    } catch (e) {\n251|        console.warn(\&quot;Could not detect variant number, using default:\&quot;, variantNumber);\n252|    }\n253|    \n254|    // Determine whether to use rotation (even) or scaling (odd)\n255|    const useRotation \u003d variantNumber % 2 \u003d\u003d\u003d 0;\n256|    \n257|    // Modify shader source with appropriate #define\n258|    let modifiedVertexSource \u003d vertexShaderSource;\n259|    if (useRotation) {\n260|        modifiedVertexSource \u003d \&quot;#define ENABLE_ROTATION\\n\&quot; + vertexShaderSource;\n261|    }\n262|    \n263|    // Continue with shader compilation...\n264|}\n265|```\n266|\n267|This approach allows the same codebase to handle both scaling and rotation variants without duplicating code.\n268|\n269|---\n270|\n271|# 4. User Instructions\n272|\n273|## 4.1 Getting Started\n274|\n275|To work with the texture transformation features, follow these steps:\n276|\n277|1. **Open the application** in a compatible web browser (Chrome, Firefox, or Edge recommended)\n278|\n279|2. **Observe the initial state** of the Cornucopia surface with the default texture mapping\n280|\n281|3. **Note the Texture Transformation panel** directly below the rendering canvas, which displays the current transformation parameters\n282|\n283|## 4.2 Keyboard Controls\n284|\n285|The texture transformations are controlled entirely through the keyboard:\n286|\n287|### Reference Point Movement\n288|- **W key**: Move reference point up (decrease v coordinate)\n289|- **S key**: Move reference point down (increase v coordinate)\n290|- **A key**: Move reference point left (decrease u coordinate)\n291|- **D key**: Move reference point right (increase u coordinate)\n292|\n293|### Transformation Controls\n294|- **Q key**: Decrease scale factor (scaling variant) \n295|- **E key**: Increase scale factor (scaling variant)\n296|- **Z key**: Rotate counter-clockwise (rotation variant)\n297|- **C key**: Rotate clockwise (rotation variant)\n298|\n299|### Reset Control\n300|- **R key**: Reset all transformation parameters to defaults\n301|\n302|## 4.3 Observing Transformations\n303|\n304|As you use the keyboard controls, observe how the texture appearance changes on the surface:\n305|\n306|### Before and After Transformation\n307|\n308|1. Before applying texture transformations:\n309|   ![Before Transformation](screenShot/screenbefore.png)\n310|\n311|2. After applying texture transformations:\n312|   ![After Transformation](screenShot/screenAfter.png)\n313|\n314|\n315|## 4.4 Tips for Effective Use\n316|\n317|- **Start with small movements** of the reference point to understand how it affects the transformation\n318|- **Observe the parameter values** in the control panel to monitor your changes\n319|- **Use the R key** to reset if the transformation becomes difficult to understand\n320|- **Experiment with different locations** of the reference point to see how it affects the transformation\u0027s appearance\n321|\n322|---\n323|\n324|# 5. Source Code Samples\n325|\n326|## 5.1 Vertex Shader with Texture Transformation\n327|\n328|```glsl\n329|// Vertex shader for Phong shading with normal mapping and texture transformation\n330|attribute vec3 vertex;\n331|attribute vec3 normal;\n332|attribute vec3 tangent;\n333|attribute vec3 bitangent;\n334|attribute vec2 texCoord;\n335|\n336|uniform mat4 ModelViewProjectionMatrix;\n337|uniform mat4 ModelViewMatrix;\n338|uniform mat3 NormalMatrix; // For transforming normals\n339|uniform vec3 lightPosition; // In world space\n340|\n341|// Texture transformation uniforms\n342|uniform vec2 texReferencePoint; // Reference point for transformations (u,v)\n343|uniform float texScaleFactor;   // Scale factor \n344|uniform float texRotationAngle; // Rotation angle in radians\n345|\n346|varying vec3 fragNormal;\n347|varying vec3 fragTangent;\n348|varying vec3 fragBitangent;\n349|varying vec3 fragPosition;\n350|varying vec3 fragLightPosition;\n351|varying vec2 fragTexCoord;\n352|\n353|// Function to transform texture coordinates based on scaling/rotation\n354|vec2 transformTexCoords(vec2 texCoords, vec2 refPoint, float scale, float angle) {\n355|    // Translate to reference point origin\n356|    vec2 centered \u003d texCoords - refPoint;\n357|    \n358|    // Apply scale and rotation\n359|    float s \u003d sin(angle);\n360|    float c \u003d cos(angle);\n361|    \n362|    // Create rotation matrix\n363|    mat2 rotation \u003d mat2(\n364|        c, -s,\n365|        s, c\n366|    );\n367|    \n368|    // Apply scale or rotation\n369|    vec2 transformed;\n370|    \n371|    #ifdef ENABLE_ROTATION\n372|        // Apply rotation around reference point\n373|        transformed \u003d rotation * centered;\n374|    #else\n375|        // Apply scaling around reference point\n376|        transformed \u003d centered * scale;\n377|    #endif\n378|    \n379|    // Translate back from reference point\n380|    return transformed + refPoint;\n381|}\n382|\n383|void main() {\n384|    // Transform vertex to clip space\n385|    gl_Position \u003d ModelViewProjectionMatrix * vec4(vertex, 1.0);\n386|    \n387|    // Pass transformed normal to fragment shader\n388|    fragNormal \u003d normalize(NormalMatrix * (-normal));\n389|    \n390|    // Transform tangent and bitangent to view space\n391|    fragTangent \u003d normalize(NormalMatrix * tangent);\n392|    fragBitangent \u003d normalize(NormalMatrix * bitangent);\n393|    \n394|    // Pass vertex position in view space for lighting calculations\n395|    fragPosition \u003d vec3(ModelViewMatrix * vec4(vertex, 1.0));\n396|    \n397|    // Pass light position to fragment shader\n398|    fragLightPosition \u003d vec3(ModelViewMatrix * vec4(lightPosition, 1.0));\n399|    \n400|    // Apply texture transformation and pass texture coordinates to fragment shader\n401|    fragTexCoord \u003d transformTexCoords(texCoord, texReferencePoint, \n402|                                      texScaleFactor, texRotationAngle);\n403|}\n404|```\n405|\n406|## 5.2 Keyboard Event Handler Implementation\n407|\n408|```javascript\n409|/**\n410| * Keyboard event handler for texture transformation controls\n411| */\n412|function setupKeyboardControls() {\n413|    document.addEventListener(\u0027keydown\u0027, function(event) {\n414|        let needRedraw \u003d true;\n415|        \n416|        switch(event.key) {\n417|            // Reference point movement\n418|            case \u0027a\u0027: case \u0027A\u0027: // Move reference point left (u-)\n419|                texReferencePoint[0] -\u003d texMoveSpeed;\n420|                break;\n421|                \n422|            case \u0027d\u0027: case \u0027D\u0027: // Move reference point right (u+)\n423|                texReferencePoint[0] +\u003d texMoveSpeed;\n424|                break;\n425|                \n426|            case \u0027w\u0027: case \u0027W\u0027: // Move reference point up (v-)\n427|                texReferencePoint[1] -\u003d texMoveSpeed;\n428|                break;\n429|                \n430|            case \u0027s\u0027: case \u0027S\u0027: // Move reference point down (v+)\n431|                texReferencePoint[1] +\u003d texMoveSpeed;\n432|                break;\n433|                \n434|            // Scaling controls\n435|            case \u0027q\u0027: case \u0027Q\u0027: // Decrease scale\n436|                texScaleFactor \u003d Math.max(0.1, texScaleFactor - 0.05);\n437|                break;\n438|                \n439|            case \u0027e\u0027: case \u0027E\u0027: // Increase scale\n440|                texScaleFactor +\u003d 0.05;\n441|                break;\n442|                \n443|            // Rotation controls\n444|            case \u0027z\u0027: case \u0027Z\u0027: // Rotate counter-clockwise\n445|                texRotationAngle -\u003d 0.05;\n446|                break;\n447|                \n448|            case \u0027c\u0027: case \u0027C\u0027: // Rotate clockwise\n449|                texRotationAngle +\u003d 0.05;\n450|                break;\n451|                \n452|            // Reset\n453|            case \u0027r\u0027: case \u0027R\u0027: // Reset transformations\n454|                texReferencePoint \u003d [0.5, 0.5];\n455|                texScaleFactor \u003d 1.0;\n456|                texRotationAngle \u003d 0.0;\n457|                break;\n458|                \n459|            default:\n460|                needRedraw \u003d false;\n461|                break;\n462|        }\n463|        \n464|        // Ensure reference point stays in reasonable bounds\n465|        texReferencePoint[0] \u003d Math.max(0.0, Math.min(1.0, texReferencePoint[0]));\n466|        texReferencePoint[1] \u003d Math.max(0.0, Math.min(1.0, texReferencePoint[1]));\n467|        \n468|        // Update UI to display current values\n469|        updateTextureControlsDisplay();\n470|        \n471|        if (needRedraw) {\n472|            // Redraw the scene with updated values\n473|            requestAnimationFrame(draw);\n474|        }\n475|    });\n476|}\n477|\n478|/**\n479| * Update the display of texture control values\n480| */\n481|function updateTextureControlsDisplay() {\n482|    // Update UI elements showing current texture control values\n483|    const refPointElem \u003d document.getElementById(\u0027tex-ref-point-value\u0027);\n484|    const scaleElem \u003d document.getElementById(\u0027tex-scale-value\u0027);\n485|    const rotationElem \u003d document.getElementById(\u0027tex-rotation-value\u0027);\n486|    \n487|    if (refPointElem) {\n488|        refPointElem.textContent \u003d `(${texReferencePoint[0].toFixed(2)}, ${texReferencePoint[1].toFixed(2)})`;\n489|    }\n490|    \n491|    if (scaleElem) {\n492|        scaleElem.textContent \u003d texScaleFactor.toFixed(2);\n493|    }\n494|    \n495|    if (rotationElem) {\n496|        // Convert to degrees for display\n497|        const degrees \u003d (texRotationAngle * 180 / Math.PI).toFixed(1);\n498|        rotationElem.textContent \u003d `${degrees}°`;\n499|    }\n500|}\n501|```\n502|\n503|## 5.3 Draw Function with Texture Transformation Uniforms\n504|\n505|```javascript\n506|/**\n507| * Draw function\n508| * @param {number} timestamp - Current timestamp from requestAnimationFrame\n509| */\n510|function draw(timestamp) {\n511|    // Apply mesh updates if needed\n512|    if (meshUpdateNeeded) {\n513|        updateMeshFromSliders();\n514|        meshUpdateNeeded \u003d false;\n515|    }\n516|    \n517|    gl.clearColor(1, 1, 1, 1);\n518|    gl.clear(gl.COLOR_BUFFER_BIT | gl.DEPTH_BUFFER_BIT);\n519|    \n520|    // Set up viewport\n521|    gl.viewport(0, 0, gl.canvas.width, gl.canvas.height);\n522|    \n523|    // Create transformation matrices\n524|    const aspect \u003d gl.canvas.width/gl.canvas.height;\n525|    const projectionMatrix \u003d m4.perspective(Math.PI/3, aspect, 1, 200);\n526|    \n527|    // Get user rotation from trackball\n528|    let modelViewMatrix \u003d spaceball.getViewMatrix();\n529|    \n530|    // Translate back to see the whole structure\n531|    const moveBack \u003d m4.translation(0, 0, -70);\n532|    \n533|    // Update light position for animation\n534|    const lightPosition \u003d updateLightPosition(timestamp);\n535|    \n536|    // Update light source object position\n537|    lightSource.updatePosition(lightPosition);\n538|    \n539|    // Apply transformations\n540|    modelViewMatrix \u003d m4.multiply(moveBack, modelViewMatrix);\n541|    \n542|    // Calculate the combined model-view-projection matrix\n543|    const mvpMatrix \u003d m4.multiply(projectionMatrix, modelViewMatrix);\n544|    \n545|    // Calculate normal matrix\n546|    const normalMatrix \u003d calculateNormalMatrix(modelViewMatrix, new Float32Array(9));\n547|    \n548|    // Set shader uniforms\n549|    gl.uniformMatrix4fv(shProgram.iModelViewProjectionMatrix, false, mvpMatrix);\n550|    gl.uniformMatrix4fv(shProgram.iModelViewMatrix, false, modelViewMatrix);\n551|    gl.uniformMatrix3fv(shProgram.iNormalMatrix, false, normalMatrix);\n552|    gl.uniform3fv(shProgram.iLightPosition, lightPosition);\n553|    gl.uniform3fv(shProgram.iLightColor, [1.0, 1.0, 1.0]);\n554|    \n555|    // Pass texture transformation uniforms\n556|    gl.uniform2fv(shProgram.iTexReferencePoint, texReferencePoint);\n557|    gl.uniform1f(shProgram.iTexScaleFactor, texScaleFactor);\n558|    gl.uniform1f(shProgram.iTexRotationAngle, texRotationAngle);\n559|    \n560|    // Draw the surface model\n561|    surface.draw(shProgram);\n562|    \n563|    // Draw the light source\n564|    lightSource.draw(shProgram, modelViewMatrix, projectionMatrix);\n565|    \n566|    // Request next frame for animation\n567|    requestAnimationFrame(draw);\n568|}\n569|```\n570|\n571|## 5.4 Variant Detection and Shader Compilation\n572|\n573|```javascript\n574|/**\n575| * Create shader program\n576| */\n577|function createProgram(gl, vertexShaderSource, fragmentShaderSource) {\n578|    // Determine the variant number\n579|    let variantNumber \u003d 23; // Default to 23\n580|    \n581|    try {\n582|        // Find all strong elements in the student info section\n583|        const studentInfo \u003d document.querySelector(\&quot;.student-info\&quot;);\n584|        if (studentInfo) {\n585|            const strongElements \u003d studentInfo.querySelectorAll(\&quot;strong\&quot;);\n586|            \n587|            // Find the one that contains \&quot;Variant\&quot;\n588|            for (let i \u003d 0; i \u003c strongElements.length; i++) {\n589|                if (strongElements[i].textContent.includes(\&quot;Variant\&quot;)) {\n590|                    // Extract the variant number using regex\n591|                    const variantText \u003d strongElements[i].parentElement.textContent;\n592|                    const match \u003d variantText.match(/Variant.*?(\\d+)/);\n593|                    if (match \u0026\u0026 match[1]) {\n594|                        variantNumber \u003d parseInt(match[1]);\n595|                        console.log(\&quot;Detected variant number:\&quot;, variantNumber);\n596|                        break;\n597|                    }\n598|                }\n599|            }\n600|        }\n601|    } catch (e) {\n602|        console.warn(\&quot;Could not detect variant number, using default:\&quot;, variantNumber);\n603|    }\n604|    \n605|    // Check if we should use rotation (even variant) or scaling (odd variant)\n606|    const useRotation \u003d variantNumber % 2 \u003d\u003d\u003d 0;\n607|    console.log(\&quot;Using \&quot; + (useRotation ? \&quot;ROTATION\&quot; : \&quot;SCALING\&quot;) + \n608|                \&quot; for texture transformation (variant \&quot; + variantNumber + \&quot;)\&quot;);\n609|    \n610|    // Modify the shader source to include the appropriate #define\n611|    let modifiedVertexSource \u003d vertexShaderSource;\n612|    if (useRotation) {\n613|        modifiedVertexSource \u003d \&quot;#define ENABLE_ROTATION\\n\&quot; + vertexShaderSource;\n614|    }\n615|    \n616|    // Create and compile vertex shader\n617|    let vsh \u003d gl.createShader(gl.VERTEX_SHADER);\n618|    gl.shaderSource(vsh, modifiedVertexSource);\n619|    gl.compileShader(vsh);\n620|    if (!gl.getShaderParameter(vsh, gl.COMPILE_STATUS)) {\n621|        throw new Error(\&quot;Error in vertex shader: \&quot; + gl.getShaderInfoLog(vsh));\n622|    }\n623|    \n624|    // Create and compile fragment shader\n625|    let fsh \u003d gl.createShader(gl.FRAGMENT_SHADER);\n626|    gl.shaderSource(fsh, fragmentShaderSource);\n627|    gl.compileShader(fsh);\n628|    if (!gl.getShaderParameter(fsh, gl.COMPILE_STATUS)) {\n629|        throw new Error(\&quot;Error in fragment shader: \&quot; + gl.getShaderInfoLog(fsh));\n630|    }\n631|    \n632|    // Create program and link shaders\n633|    let prog \u003d gl.createProgram();\n634|    gl.attachShader(prog, vsh);\n635|    gl.attachShader(prog, fsh);\n636|    gl.linkProgram(prog);\n637|    if (!gl.getProgramParameter(prog, gl.LINK_STATUS)) {\n638|        throw new Error(\&quot;Link error in program: \&quot; + gl.getProgramInfoLog(prog));\n639|    }\n640|    \n641|    return prog;\n642|}\n643|```\n644|\n645|This completes the source code section, providing key examples from the implementation that highlight the texture transformation functionality.&quot;},{&quot;role&quot;:&quot;assistant&quot;,&quot;content&quot;:[],&quot;tool_calls&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;toolu_01GxM7ZyRDdgrgLMCzEZrmj7&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;function&quot;,&quot;function&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;edit&quot;,&quot;arguments&quot;:&quot;{\&quot;file_path\&quot;: \&quot;D:/PROJECT_ME/project_SHAME/INSTITUTKA/Visualisation/WebGL2/README.md\&quot;, \&quot;old_string\&quot;: \&quot;# WebGL Texture Transformation Project\\n\\n## Cornucopia Surface with Dynamic Texture Mapping\\n\\n![Before Transformation](screenShot/screenbefore.png)\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1. Task Description\&quot;, \&quot;new_string\&quot;: \&quot;# НАЦІОНАЛЬНИЙ ТЕХНІЧНИЙ УНІВЕРСИТЕТ УКРАЇНИ \\\&quot;КИЇВСЬКИЙ ПОЛІТЕХНІЧНИЙ ІНСТИТУТ ІМЕНІ ІГОРЯ СІКОРСЬКОГО\\\&quot;\\n\\n## НАВЧАЛЬНО-НАУКОВИХ ІНСТИТУТ АТОМНОЇ ТА ТЕПЛОВОЇ ЕНЕРГЕТИКИ\\n\\n### КАФЕДРА ЦИФРОВИХ ТЕХНОЛОГІЙ В ЕНЕРГЕТИЦІ\\n\\n---\\n\\n# ЛАБОРАТОРНА РОБОТА №2\\nз дисципліни \\\&quot;Проектування систем з розподіленими базами даних в енергетиці\\\&quot;\\n\\n## ТЕМА: \\\&quot;Основи Cassandra: створення першої енергетичної системи моніторингу\\\&quot;\\n\\n### ВАРІАНТ: 23\\n\\nВиконав: студент групи ТР-51мп Пащенко Микола Ігорович\\n\\nПеревірив: Демчишин Анатолій Анатолійович\\n\\nКиїв – 2025\\n\\n---\\n\\n# WebGL Texture Transformation Project\\n\\n## Cornuc